# Agent model for machines running models.big.conf (48GB+ of RAM).
#
#   ollama create agent-big -f modelfiles/agent-big.Modelfile
#   (setup-local-ai.sh builds this automatically when the base is local)
#
# WHY A SECOND FILE: agent.Modelfile is FROM qwen2.5-coder:14b, which only
# models.conf pulls. On a big machine that base is never downloaded, so that
# modelfile is skipped and the num_ctx fix below never gets applied — the one
# thing these files exist for. This one is FROM a base that models.big.conf
# actually pulls.
#
# Only the file whose FROM base is present gets built. setup-local-ai.sh skips
# the other with "⊘ needs base", which is correct rather than an error: you do
# not want it silently pulling a base you did not ask for.
#
# ── the problem both files solve ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Ollama defaults num_ctx to roughly 2-4K tokens. An agent blows past that
# within a few tool calls. When it overflows, Ollama does NOT error: it
# SILENTLY TRUNCATES. The agent forgets its own plan, re-reads files it just
# read, and loops. It looks like a stupid model. It has amnesia.
#
# ── the cost: KV cache is RAM ────────────────────────────────────────────
# devstral:24b is ~14GB of weights. At q8_0 its KV cache is roughly
# ~130KB/token, so on top of the weights:
#
#     32K ctx  ~= 4GB    -> ~18GB resident. Comfortable in a 34GB big slot.
#     64K ctx  ~= 8GB    -> ~22GB. Fits, but raise the GPU cap first.
#    128K ctx  ~= 16GB   -> ~30GB. Only with the cap raised and little else.
#
# 32K is a good default here: enough for real multi-file work, and it leaves
# the slot some room. Check reality rather than trusting the arithmetic:
#     ./scripts/model.sh load agent-big && ./scripts/ram.sh

FROM devstral:24b

PARAMETER num_ctx 32768
PARAMETER temperature 0.1
PARAMETER top_p 0.9

# Agents need determinism more than flair — they follow a plan rather than
# writing prose, and low temperature also reduces malformed tool calls.

SYSTEM """
You are a coding agent operating on a real repository.

Read before you write. Prefer the smallest change that solves the problem.
Follow the existing style of the file you are editing.
State assumptions explicitly. If you are unsure, say so rather than inventing
an API or a file path.
After editing, verify: run the test or the command that proves it works.
"""
